{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"=== HARNESS RUN 3 (30721408463, job 91425720889, main @ 331cfb7e88): 29 passed, 19 failed. ===\nREAD THE BREAKDOWN BEFORE THE COUNT — \"19 failed\" is NOT 19 broken cells.\n\nFIRST, WHAT DID NOT BREAK. **All 26 pre-existing cells PASSED**, including\n`test_a_local_edit_reaches_the_dc_issue_outbound` and\n`test_a_dc_issue_reaches_the_local_store_inbound`. So the epic's headline AC remains proven, and\nthe conftest/`_dc_support` refactor (moving `dc_store_copy_repo`, `bound_dc_issue`, `dc_request`\ninto conftest so two modules can share them) broke nothing. Three NEW cells also passed:\n`test_outbound_link_round_trips`, `test_set_parent_declines_the_epic_case_loudly`, and\n`test_no_config_in_the_working_repo_points_anywhere_but_the_harness`.\n\n*** 13 OF THE 19 FAILED ONLY ON MY BUNDLED IDEMPOTENCE ASSERTION — THEIR ROUND-TRIP ORACLES\nPASSED. *** inbound 03-description, 05-add-label, 06-remove-label, 07-comment, 08-assign,\n09-unassign; outbound 02-title, 03-description, 05-add-label, 07-comment, 08-assign, and\nremove-label. Every one of those mutations DID round-trip; the cell then ran\n`_assert_repeat_pass_is_a_noop` and that is what fired.\n**This is my own lesson, violated by me.** \"When an assertion can fail for two reasons, split it\ninto two cells.\" I bundled idempotence into every round-trip cell, so thirteen working mutations\nreport as failures and the real signal is buried. The remedy is structural, not a tweak: hoist\nidempotence into its OWN cell(s).\nOn the merits, the repeat entry is `{'direction': 'outbound', 'action': 'update', 'target':\n'<KEY>', 'local_id': '<KEY>'}`. The likeliest cause is INDEX LAG, not churn: the differ reads the\nremote snapshot through a JQL search, and I never waited for the index to reflect the outbound\nwrite before re-planning — the inbound cells wait via `_wait_until_search_reflects`, the repeat\ncheck does not. Until that wait is added the check cannot distinguish lag from real churn, so it\nmust not be read as evidence of either.\n\n*** A TEST-FILTER BUG THAT WOULD HAVE BEEN A SERIOUS FALSE ALARM ***\n`test_the_inbound_snapshot_survives_multi_page_pagination` reported \"recovered only 0 of 201\nseeded issues — 201 were silently LOST\". **That is NOT data loss; it is my filter.** The plan\nenvelope's `local_id` field is populated from `provenance[\"local_id\"]`\n(`reconcile_helpers._build_plan_entries:466`) and for these entries it carries the JIRA KEY — the\nfailure output above shows `'local_id': 'RBJISZB-1'` on an outbound update. My pagination cell\nmatched `_jira_key_to_local_id(key)` against `local_id` ONLY, so it matched nothing at all, which\nis why the number is a suspiciously round 0 rather than a partial. The thin slice's own\n`test_the_inbound_create_is_PLANNED_for_a_new_dc_issue` gets this right and passes — it ORs on\n`target`. Fix the filter to match `target`, as that cell does.\nRecording this loudly because \"the inbound fetch lost 201 of 201 issues\" is exactly the kind of\nfinding that would have sent the next session hunting a pagination defect that is not there.\n\n*** THE GENUINELY OPEN FINDINGS — five, each needing its own investigation ***\n 1. `inbound 02-edit-summary` — the new summary did NOT reach the local ticket (`.title` stayed at\n    the fixture's value) while `03-edit-description` DID reach it on the same code path. A\n    title-specific difference; do not assume it generalises to \"inbound updates are broken\".\n 2. `outbound 04-transition-status` — the status did not reach DC. Note the harness project's\n    workflow may not offer the \"In Progress\" transition from the issue's current state;\n    `transition_issue_by_name` raises with the available list, so check whether the pass swallowed\n    that before concluding the bridge is at fault.\n 3. `test_inbound_link_round_trips` — the Jira link did not surface as a local dep.\n 4. `test_a_deleted_dc_issue_is_planned_as_an_inbound_probe` — deleting the issue planned no\n    inbound probe.\n 5. `test_the_inbound_assignee_mints_a_jira_family_identity` — `.assignee` is the RAW DC username\n    `'admin'`, not a minted rebar identity id. This is a real observation about the inbound\n    identity path and is the most interesting of the five: it means the inbound assignee is stored\n    verbatim rather than resolved through the identity registry, which is what bug 5f48 was\n    circling. Worth its own ticket rather than a test tweak.\n\nNEXT ACTIONS, in order: (a) split idempotence out of the round-trip cells and give it an\nindex-lag wait; (b) fix the pagination cell to match on `target`; (c) then triage the five above\non a clean run, where they will no longer be buried under thirteen false reds."},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1785626268007691001,"uuid":"8222e62b-a0ed-4e1f-b33f-09c1c0694c12"}